This code calculates the number of unique paths from the top-left corner to the bottom-right corner of an m x n grid, where movement is only allowed downwards or rightwards. It uses a 2D DP table where dp[i][j] represents the number of ways to reach cell (i,j). The first row and first column are initialized to 1 (only one way to reach any cell along the top or left edge). For all other cells, the recurrence dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j] + dp[i][j-1] is applied, summing the ways from the top and left neighbors. Finally, the value at dp[m-1][n-1] is returned.

Time Complexity: O(m × n) — the nested loops iterate over all cells once.
Space Complexity: O(m × n) — for the DP table (though this can be optimized to O(n), the given implementation uses 2D).

class Solution {
    public int uniquePaths(int m, int n) {
        int[][] dp = new int[m][n];
        for(int i = 0; i<n; i++){
            dp[0][i] = 1;
        }
        for(int i = 0; i<m; i++){
            dp[i][0] = 1;
        }
        for(int i = 1; i<m; i++){
            for(int j = 1; j<n; j++){
                dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j] + dp[i][j-1];
            }
        }
        return dp[m-1][n-1];
    }
}
